EN_C = [
("Crop Insurance", "Farming in India is exposed to many risks, including drought, floods, hailstorms, pest attacks and sudden price falls. A single bad season can push a farming family into debt. Crop insurance is designed to protect farmers against such losses. Under the national crop insurance scheme, farmers pay a small share of the premium, while the rest is paid by the central and state governments. If yields fall below a certain level because of natural causes, the farmer receives compensation directly in the bank account. The use of satellites, drones and smartphones for estimating crop damage has made the assessment faster and more transparent. Still, many farmers complain about delays in claim settlement and lack of information about the process. Tenant farmers, who cultivate land owned by others, often find it difficult to enrol. Simplifying procedures, setting strict timelines for payment and spreading awareness at the village level can make insurance a real safety net for farmers."),
("Smart Classrooms", "Smart classrooms use technology such as interactive boards, projectors, tablets and educational software to make teaching more engaging. A difficult topic like the structure of an atom or the working of the heart can be explained through animations and three dimensional models that students can see and understand easily. Teachers can show short videos, conduct quick online quizzes and get instant feedback on how much the class has understood. Many government schools have also received smart classroom equipment under various schemes. However, technology alone does not guarantee better learning. If teachers are not trained to use these tools effectively, expensive devices may remain unused or serve only as a substitute for the blackboard. Regular electricity, internet access and technical support are equally important. The best results are seen where technology is used to support good teaching, not to replace it, and where students are encouraged to think, discuss and create rather than simply watch."),
("The Indian Navy", "The Indian Navy protects the country's long coastline, its island territories and its vast maritime trade routes. Most of India's trade by volume travels by sea, so a strong navy is essential for economic security as well as defence. The navy operates aircraft carriers, destroyers, frigates, submarines and maritime patrol aircraft. In recent years, a growing number of these warships have been designed and built in Indian shipyards, reflecting the drive for self reliance in defence production. Apart from its combat role, the navy carries out humanitarian missions. It has evacuated Indian citizens from conflict zones abroad, delivered relief material after cyclones and tsunamis, and rescued fishermen and sailors in distress. The navy also conducts joint exercises with friendly countries to improve cooperation and to keep the sea lanes of the Indian Ocean safe from piracy and other threats. Navy Day is celebrated every year on 4 December."),
("Plastic Waste and Its Solutions", "Plastic is light, strong and cheap, and so it is found in almost everything, from milk pouches to chairs. But the same durability that makes it useful also makes it a serious environmental problem. Plastic can take hundreds of years to break down, and much of it ends up in drains, rivers and oceans, where it harms fish, birds and other animals. Tiny fragments called microplastics have even been found in drinking water and food. India has banned several single use plastic items such as plastic straws, cutlery and thin carry bags. Producers of packaging are now responsible for collecting and recycling a part of the plastic they put into the market. Citizens can contribute by carrying cloth bags, refusing unnecessary packaging and segregating waste at home so that plastic can be recycled. Some cities are using waste plastic in road construction, which shows that, with the right systems, plastic waste can become a useful resource."),
("Mental Health at Work", "Long working hours, tight deadlines, job insecurity and the constant pressure of being connected through phones and emails are taking a toll on the mental health of employees. Stress, anxiety and burnout not only affect personal wellbeing but also reduce productivity and increase absenteeism. Many workers hesitate to talk about their mental health for fear of being judged or losing opportunities. Forward looking organisations are taking steps to address this issue. They offer confidential counselling services, train managers to recognise signs of distress and encourage employees to take regular breaks and use their leave. Some companies have introduced policies that discourage sending work messages after office hours. Simple changes, such as reasonable workloads, clear communication and appreciation for good work, can make a big difference. Employees, too, can protect themselves by maintaining a balance between work and personal life, exercising regularly and seeking help without hesitation when they feel overwhelmed."),
("Food Fortification", "Food fortification means adding essential vitamins and minerals to commonly eaten foods to improve their nutritional value. In India, a large number of people, especially women and children, suffer from deficiencies of iron, iodine, vitamin A and vitamin D, even when they eat enough food. These hidden forms of hunger can lead to anaemia, weak immunity, poor growth and reduced ability to learn and work. One of the most successful examples of fortification is the use of iodised salt, which has greatly reduced iodine deficiency disorders. The government is now supplying fortified rice through the public distribution system, school meals and nutrition programmes. Edible oil and milk are also being fortified with vitamins A and D. Experts say that fortification is a cost effective way to reach large populations, but it must be combined with a diverse diet, safe drinking water and good sanitation. Regular testing is necessary to ensure that the right quantities of nutrients are added."),
("Old Age and Loneliness", "As families become smaller and young people move to distant cities or abroad for work, a growing number of elderly people in India are living alone. Many of them face loneliness, which can be as harmful to health as physical illness. Without regular company, older people may lose interest in daily activities, eat poorly and become depressed. Chronic diseases, limited mobility and financial dependence add to their difficulties. Communities can do a great deal to support senior citizens. Day care centres, where elderly people can spend time together, play games, read and share meals, have proved very helpful. Neighbourhood associations can keep a list of residents who live alone and check on them regularly. Younger family members, even when far away, can stay connected through regular phone and video calls. Laws also require children to provide maintenance to their parents. Respect and care for the elderly reflect the values of a civilised society."),
("Inland Fisheries", "Fish is an affordable source of protein for millions of Indians, and fisheries provide a livelihood to a large population, many of whom are poor. While marine fishing is well known, inland fisheries in rivers, reservoirs, ponds and tanks now contribute the larger share of total fish production in the country. States like Andhra Pradesh, West Bengal and Odisha are leading producers. Farmers are increasingly adopting scientific methods, such as using quality fish seed, balanced feed and aeration systems, which have greatly increased yields. Integrated farming, in which fish are raised along with ducks, poultry or paddy, helps farmers earn more from the same piece of land. A dedicated scheme supports the construction of new ponds, cold storage and fish markets, and provides insurance to fishers. Challenges remain in the form of water pollution, disease outbreaks and poor market access. With proper planning, inland fisheries can boost rural incomes and improve nutrition."),
("Lessons from the Pandemic", "The COVID 19 pandemic tested every part of society and exposed both strengths and weaknesses. Hospitals were overwhelmed, oxygen and beds were in short supply during the worst waves, and millions of workers lost their livelihoods during lockdowns. At the same time, the crisis showed what can be achieved when science, government and citizens work together. Vaccines were developed in record time, and India carried out one of the largest vaccination drives in history using a digital platform for registration and certificates. Teachers learned to teach online, doctors began consulting through video calls and many businesses shifted to digital payments. The pandemic also taught important lessons. Public health systems need steady investment, not just emergency funds. Reliable data and clear communication build trust. Social security for informal workers is essential to protect them during sudden shocks. Above all, preparedness for future health emergencies must be treated as a national priority."),
("Bamboo as a Resource", "Bamboo is often called green gold because of its many uses and its remarkable rate of growth. Some species can grow nearly a metre in a single day, and a bamboo clump can be harvested every few years without killing the plant. It is used to make houses, furniture, scaffolding, baskets, paper, fabrics and even bicycles. Bamboo shoots are eaten as a vegetable in many parts of the northeast. Because it grows quickly, bamboo absorbs a large amount of carbon dioxide and helps prevent soil erosion on slopes and riverbanks. For a long time, bamboo grown outside forests was legally treated as a tree, which made its cutting and transport difficult. A change in the law removed this restriction, encouraging farmers to grow it on their land. Training artisans in modern design and connecting them with markets can turn bamboo into a major source of income for rural and tribal communities."),
("Consumer Rights", "Every consumer has the right to get goods and services of good quality at a fair price. The Consumer Protection Act gives buyers several important rights, including the right to safety, the right to be informed, the right to choose and the right to seek redressal. If a product is defective or a service is deficient, the consumer can file a complaint before a consumer commission at the district, state or national level, depending on the value of the claim. Complaints can now be filed online, and a consumer does not always need a lawyer. The law also covers online shopping and holds e commerce platforms responsible for unfair practices. Misleading advertisements can attract heavy penalties, and celebrities who endorse such products may also be held accountable. To make full use of these rights, consumers should always ask for a proper bill, check expiry dates and quality marks, and keep records of warranties and transactions."),
("The Role of Cooperative Dairies", "India produces more milk than any other country, and cooperative dairies have played a central role in this achievement. Under the cooperative model, small farmers who own just one or two animals become members of a village society. They pour milk at the collection centre twice a day, where its fat content is tested and payment is credited regularly. The milk is then chilled, processed and sold under a common brand. Because the cooperative is owned by the producers themselves, a large share of the consumer price reaches the farmers. The movement that began in Anand in Gujarat inspired similar unions in many states. Dairying is especially important for landless families and women, who do most of the work of caring for animals. Challenges today include low productivity of animals, shortage of green fodder and the need for better veterinary care. Improving breeds and feed management can raise farmers' incomes further."),
("Light Pollution", "When we think of pollution, we usually think of smoke, dirty water or noise. Light pollution, which is the excessive or poorly directed use of artificial light at night, receives far less attention. In most cities, the night sky is so bright that only a handful of stars can be seen. This loss of darkness affects more than astronomers. Many birds that migrate at night use the stars to navigate, and bright lights can confuse them, causing collisions with buildings. Sea turtle hatchlings that should move towards the moonlit sea may crawl towards street lights instead. Artificial light at night can also disturb the natural sleep cycle of human beings and affect their health. The solutions are simple and often save money. Street lights can be shielded so that they shine downward, warmer lights can be used and unnecessary lights can be switched off late at night. Some places have even created dark sky reserves to protect the natural night."),
("Skill India Mission", "India has one of the youngest populations in the world, but many young people lack the practical skills that employers need. The Skill India Mission was launched to close this gap by training millions of youth in trades such as electrical work, plumbing, beauty care, retail, healthcare and information technology. Training is provided through industrial training institutes, private centres and apprenticeships with companies. After completing a course, trainees receive a certificate that is recognised by employers across the country. Recognition of prior learning allows workers who learned a trade informally, such as masons and tailors, to get their skills certified. Despite these efforts, the placement rate of many training programmes has remained low. Experts suggest that industry should be closely involved in designing courses, that training should be linked to local job opportunities and that more emphasis should be placed on apprenticeships, where learners earn while they acquire skills on the job."),
("Tiger Conservation", "When Project Tiger was launched in 1973, the tiger population in India had fallen to alarmingly low levels because of hunting and loss of habitat. Since then, the number of tiger reserves has grown steadily, and India today is home to the majority of the world's wild tigers. Tiger censuses, conducted every four years using camera traps and scientific methods, have shown a steady increase in numbers. Protecting tigers also protects the entire forest ecosystem, including rivers, grasslands and countless other species. However, success has brought new challenges. As tiger numbers grow, some animals move out of reserves and come into conflict with villagers, killing cattle and occasionally people. Creating safe corridors between forests, compensating villagers promptly for losses and involving local communities in conservation are essential. Eco tourism, if properly regulated, can provide jobs to local people and give them a stake in protecting the forest and its wildlife."),
("Election Commission of India", "The Election Commission of India is a constitutional body responsible for conducting free and fair polls for the Lok Sabha, the Rajya Sabha, state assemblies and the posts of President and Vice President. It was established on 25 January 1950, a day now celebrated as National Voters Day. The Commission prepares electoral rolls, announces election schedules, recognises political parties and allots symbols. Once elections are announced, a model code of conduct comes into force, which restricts governments from announcing new schemes and guides the behaviour of parties and candidates. The use of electronic voting machines and voter verifiable paper audit trails has made counting faster and more reliable. Conducting elections in a country as large as India is a huge task, involving millions of polling staff and security personnel, and polling stations in remote forests, deserts and mountains. The Commission also runs awareness campaigns to encourage every eligible citizen, especially young and first time voters, to register and vote."),
("Healthy Eating Habits", "Our eating habits have changed rapidly over the past few decades. Home cooked meals made from grains, pulses and vegetables are increasingly being replaced by packaged snacks, sugary drinks and fast food that are high in salt, sugar and unhealthy fats. As a result, lifestyle diseases such as obesity, diabetes and heart disease are rising even among young people. Nutrition experts recommend a balanced plate that includes whole grains, pulses, seasonal vegetables and fruits, milk or curd, and a small amount of nuts and healthy oils. Eating meals at regular times, chewing food properly and drinking enough water are equally important. Reading labels on packaged foods can help people avoid products that contain excessive sugar or trans fats. Schools can encourage healthy eating by restricting junk food in canteens and teaching children about nutrition. Small changes, made consistently, can protect health and reduce the burden of disease on families and the country."),
("The Great Indian Peninsula", "The peninsular plateau is the oldest and most stable landmass of the Indian subcontinent. It is shaped roughly like a triangle, bounded by the Western Ghats on the west, the Eastern Ghats on the east and the Vindhya and Satpura ranges in the north. The plateau is made of ancient crystalline rocks and is rich in minerals such as iron ore, coal, manganese, bauxite and mica. Major rivers like the Godavari, Krishna and Kaveri rise in the Western Ghats and flow eastwards into the Bay of Bengal, while the Narmada and Tapi flow westwards through rift valleys into the Arabian Sea. The black soil of the Deccan, formed from volcanic rocks, is ideal for growing cotton. The Western Ghats receive heavy rainfall and are recognised as one of the world's biodiversity hotspots. Understanding the physical geography of this region helps explain the distribution of crops, industries and population in southern and central India."),
("Online Learning Platforms", "Online learning platforms have made quality education accessible to anyone with an internet connection. A student in a small town can now attend lectures by experienced professors, practise problems and take tests at his or her own pace. Government platforms offer free courses in a wide range of subjects, and credits earned through some of them can be counted towards university degrees. Working professionals use online courses to learn new skills without leaving their jobs. However, online learning has its limitations. Many students find it difficult to stay motivated without the discipline of a classroom and the support of classmates. Poor connectivity and the lack of personal devices exclude a section of learners. Practical subjects that require laboratories and hands on training cannot be fully taught online. A blended approach, which combines online resources with classroom teaching and personal mentoring, is increasingly seen as the most effective model for the future of education."),
("The Spirit of Sportsmanship", "Sportsmanship is the attitude of playing fairly, respecting opponents and accepting both victory and defeat with dignity. It is often said that the true value of sport lies not only in winning medals but in the character it builds. Many memorable moments in sporting history are remembered not for the result but for an act of kindness or honesty, such as an athlete stopping to help a fallen rival or a cricketer walking off the field without waiting for the umpire's decision. Good sportsmanship includes following the rules, avoiding the use of banned substances, controlling anger and congratulating the winner sincerely. Coaches and parents play a key role in teaching these values to young players. When they focus only on results, children may learn to cheat or to fear failure. When they praise effort, discipline and fair play, children grow into athletes and citizens who understand that how you play matters as much as whether you win."),
]
